[[ja:大祓]]

{{Short description|Japanese purification rite and festival}}
{{Copyedit|date=November 2023}}
{{Expand Japanese|大祓|date=November 2023}}
{{Japanese Imperial Rituals}}
'''Ōharae-shiki''' is a Shinto ritual also known as the Great Purification.<ref>https://www.senninso.com/oharae-thegreatpurification/</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Namiki |first=Kazuko |title=Ōharae |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9119 |access-date=November 9th 2023 |website=[[Kokugakuin University]] Encyclopedia of Shinto}}</ref>. The name Oharae literally means Great [[Harae]]<ref name=":0" />

The ritual was held biannually as a festival at the end of the sixth and twelfth months, but also on an ad hoc basis whenever a great purification was needed, such as after someone commits an offense, or a [[Daijosai]] or an unmarried prince or princess visiting [[Ise Jingu]] or the [[Kamo Shrine|Kamo Shrines]].<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1" />{{rp|7}}

It is hosted annually on June 30th.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nagoshi no Oharae Purification Ritual |url=https://www.kamigamojinja.jp/en/rituals/nagoshi-no-oharae-purification-ritual/ |access-date=2023-06-05 |website=Kamo Wakeikazuchi Jinja Shrine (Kamigamo Jinja) |language=ja}}</ref> It is also hosted on December 30th.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Meador |first=Spencer |date=2017-07-04 |title=Oharae |url=http://sanpai-japan.com/2017/07/04/oharae/ |access-date=2023-06-05 |website=Sanpai Japan |language=en-US}}</ref> The last one is sometimes done on New years eve instead.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Shiwasu Oharae-shiki (Great Purification Ceremony in December) {{!}} Samukawa-jinja Shrine |url=https://samukawajinjya.jp/en/festival/shiwasuoharae.html |access-date=2023-06-05 |website=Samukawa-jinja Shrine Official Website |language=en}}</ref>

The Engishiki specified imperial involvement with four festivals, the {{ill|Kinen-sai|ja|祈年祭}}, the two Ōharae-shiki and [[Niiname-no-Matsuri]] for tribute.<ref name=":1">{{Cite book |last=Hardacre |first=Helen |url=https://www.amazon.ca/Shinto-History-Helen-Hardacre/dp/0190621710 |title=Shinto: A History |date=2016-11-11 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=978-0-19-062171-1 |edition=Illustrated edition |location=New York (N.Y.) |language=English}}</ref>{{rp|36}}

[[Oharae no Kotoba]] are prayers used in this process.<ref>https://www.ookunitamajinja.or.jp/pdf/202012_OHARAE_3.pdf</ref>

It is also done at [[Isonokami Shrine]]

== Historical Changes ==
Histoorians believe that the regular Ōharae rites started at the end of the [[7th century|seventh century]]. They changed a lot over time, especially until the [[15th century|fifteenth century]]. These changes were linked to updates in the [[Ritsuryō|ritsuryō system]], a set of laws and rules, in the eighth century.<ref name=":0" />

In the [[Edo period]] (1603–1868), Ōharae changed again. It was different from what it was in medieval times. In the [[Meiji era|Meiji period]] (1868–1912), some parts of the old rite were brought back.<ref name=":0" />

Purification became more important leading to this ritual and {{ill|Yoori|ja|節折}} becoming more prominent in the Meiji restoration.<ref name=":1" />{{rp|362}}

== Different from Minazuki-barae ==
People often mix up the Ōharae in the sixth month with Minazuki-barae (水無月祓), another purification rite at the same time. But history shows they were different. Each had its own purpose and importance in the court's rituals.<ref name=":0" />

