The Engishiki specified imperial involvement with four festivals, the [[Kinen-sai]], the two Ōharae-shiki and [[Niiname-no-Matsuri]] for tribute.<ref name="Hardacre-2016">{{Cite book |last=Hardacre |first=Helen |title=Shinto: A History |date=2016-11-11 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=978-0-19-062171-1 |edition=Illustrated |location=New York (N.Y.) |language=English}}</ref>{{rp|36}}

[[Oharae no Kotoba]] are prayers used in this process.<ref>https://www.ookunitamajinja.or.jp/pdf/202012_OHARAE_3.pdf</ref>

It is also done at [[Isonokami Shrine]]

== Historical changes ==
Histoorians believe that the regular Ōharae rites started at the end of the [[7th century|seventh century]]. They changed a lot over time, especially until the [[15th century|fifteenth century]]. These changes were linked to updates in the [[Ritsuryō|ritsuryō system]], a set of laws and rules, in the eighth century.<ref name="Namiki" />

In the [[Edo period]] (1603–1868), Ōharae changed again. It was different from what it was in medieval times. In the [[Meiji era|Meiji period]] (1868–1912), some parts of the old rite were brought back.<ref name="Namiki" />

Purification became more important leading to this ritual and {{ill|Yoori|ja|節折}} becoming more prominent in the Meiji restoration.<ref name="Hardacre-2016" />{{rp|362}}

== Different from Minazuki-barae ==
People often mix up the Ōharae in the sixth month with Minazuki-barae (水無月祓), another purification rite at the same time. But history shows they were different. Each had its own purpose and importance in the court's rituals.<ref name="Namiki" />

